        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-full",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "DORA applies in full",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The organisation running this system is a financial entity under the full framework of the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), applying since 17 January 2025; its ICT risk management follows DORA Articles 5 to 15 and Title II of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1774, kept by the compliance function. For this system: ICT-related incidents are recorded in the incident tool (incident.io, PagerDuty or Jira Service Management, with custom fields for the criteria of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1772) and classified within {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} hours; major ones are reported to {{AUTHORITY}} on the templates of Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302 within the time limits of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301, filed by {{REPORTING}}. The register of information: {{REGISTER}}. Systems supporting critical or important functions are tested at least every {{TEST_MONTHS}} months; the entity is {{TLPT}}. What other packs decide is only referred to here: change management, segregation of duties and emergency changes in the Definition of Done and change approval pack; backups and restore drills in Backup and recovery; availability targets in Availability; monitoring and paging in Observability; dependencies and the SBOM in Software supply chain; encryption in Encryption; audit records in Audit trail.",
      "consequences": "• Every major incident costs three reports, the first within hours, day or night: someone able to file must always be reachable.\n• A new integration in a pull request needs a contract reference before it merges, which slows down trying a new SaaS.\n• Yearly resilience tests and their remediation dates become part of the plan, with independent testers to pay for.\n• The records live in the repository beside the code, so the supervisor's questions are answered from the same source the engineers change.\n• The legal reading stays with the compliance officer; the pack covers only what the system and its team must show.",
      "alternatives": "1. The simplified framework of Art. 16: less ICT risk management to document, but only for the entities Art. 16(1) lists.\n2. DORA as a yearly compliance project outside engineering: cheaper day to day, but the register and the runbook drift from the system between audits.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-simplified",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "DORA applies with the simplified framework",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The organisation running this system is a financial entity under the simplified ICT risk management framework of the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), applying since 17 January 2025: Art. 16 and Title III of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1774 replace Articles 5 to 15, kept by the compliance function. Incident reporting, the register of information and resilience testing apply as to any financial entity, and threat-led penetration testing does not (Art. 26(1)). For this system: ICT-related incidents are recorded in the incident tool (incident.io, PagerDuty or Jira Service Management, with custom fields for the criteria of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1772) and classified within {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} hours; major ones are reported to {{AUTHORITY}} on the templates of Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302 within the time limits of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301, filed by {{REPORTING}}. The register of information: {{REGISTER}}. Systems supporting critical or important functions are tested at least every {{TEST_MONTHS}} months. What other packs decide is only referred to here: change management, segregation of duties and emergency changes in the Definition of Done and change approval pack; backups and restore drills in Backup and recovery; availability targets in Availability; monitoring and paging in Observability; dependencies and the SBOM in Software supply chain; encryption in Encryption; audit records in Audit trail.",
      "consequences": "• Less to document for ICT risk management, but the same incident deadlines as the largest bank.\n• A new integration in a pull request needs a contract reference before it merges.\n• No threat-led penetration testing; yearly resilience tests still need independent testers.\n• Growth or a change of licence can end the simplified framework: the scope review has to catch it.",
      "alternatives": "1. The full framework anyway: more documentation than DORA asks, a head start if the entity grows out of Art. 16(1).\n2. Treating a small entity as out of scope: Art. 16 eases the framework, it does not remove reporting, the register or testing.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "simplified"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-provider",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "DORA reaches us through our clients",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The organisation provides ICT services to financial entities and is not one itself: the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554) reaches it through its clients' contracts (Art. 30) until the ESAs designate it critical under Art. 31, which puts it under a Lead Overseer and supersedes this decision. The team offers a DORA addendum at contracts/dora-addendum.md with each clause of Art. 30(2) and (3); keeps the list of its ICT subcontractors at compliance/dora/subcontractors.yaml, checked against the external systems in the architecture model, for the clients' registers; tells affected clients of an incident within {{CLIENT_NOTICE_HOURS}} hours with the facts they need to classify it; and takes part in their threat-led penetration tests where a contract for a critical or important function asks. What other packs decide is only referred to here: change management, segregation of duties and emergency changes in the Definition of Done and change approval pack; backups and restore drills in Backup and recovery; availability targets in Availability; monitoring and paging in Observability; dependencies and the SBOM in Software supply chain; encryption in Encryption; audit records in Audit trail.",
      "consequences": "• Clients, their auditors and their authorities may inspect and audit the service (Art. 30(3)(e)), which needs a prepared evidence room.\n• An incident notice within {{CLIENT_NOTICE_HOURS}} hours needs someone on call who can write it, at any hour.\n• Adding or changing a subcontractor becomes a notice to clients, not a purely technical change.\n• One addendum for all clients keeps one set of promises; clients with stronger demands still negotiate.\n• Designation as critical would bring direct oversight; this decision is superseded the day the ESAs designate the provider.",
      "alternatives": "1. Negotiating DORA clauses client by client: no standard to maintain, and as many different promises as clients.\n2. Treating DORA as the clients' problem: their contracts still bind the provider, and a provider that cannot show the clauses loses regulated clients.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "provider"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-none",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Outside DORA",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack. Here the compliance officer has concluded that none of it applies to the organisation.",
      "decision": "The EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554) does not apply to the organisation running this system: it is not a financial entity of Art. 2(1)(a) to (t), or is excluded by Art. 2(3), and provides no ICT services to financial entities. compliance/dora/scope.yaml records the basis and who reviewed it; the record is reviewed at least every {{SCOPE_REVIEW_MONTHS}} months, and applying for a financial licence or signing a financial-entity client supersedes this decision.",
      "consequences": "• No incident reports, register or resilience-testing cycle are owed under DORA.\n• A new client in the financial sector can bring DORA in through its contract overnight; sales must tell the compliance officer.\n• The scope record and its review are the only cost, and the only evidence if anyone asks.",
      "alternatives": "1. Following DORA voluntarily: good practice for incident handling and supplier lists, at a cost nobody requires.\n2. Recording nothing: cheaper today, and no basis to point to when a client or an authority asks.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "none"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-tlpt",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Threat-led penetration testing on the authority's cycle",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The competent authority has named the entity for threat-led penetration testing: at least every {{TLPT_MONTHS}} months a test covering the critical or important functions it names runs on the live production systems supporting them, following Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/1190 (built on the TIBER-EU framework), with threat intelligence from outside the entity and testers meeting Art. 27 (internal testers only with the authority's approval, Art. 27(2)); the ICT third-party providers of those functions take part, as their contracts require (Art. 30(3)(d)). The end date of the last test, its attestation and the functions it covered are kept in compliance/dora/scope.yaml under tlpt, and the remediation plan is tracked with the resilience-test findings.",
      "consequences": "• A test takes months of preparation with a threat-intelligence provider and a red team, and a budget to match.\n• Live production is attacked on purpose: the test needs a control team and a way to stop it.\n• Findings and the remediation plan go to the authority (Art. 26(6)), so they are fixed on a schedule the authority sees.",
      "alternatives": "1. Ordinary yearly penetration tests only: they do not meet Art. 26 once the entity is named.\n2. Internal testers only: possible solely with the authority's approval and external threat intelligence (Art. 27(2)).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full"
        ],
        "TLPT": [
          "named"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-no-tlpt",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Not named for threat-led penetration testing",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 650,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The competent authority has not named the entity for threat-led penetration testing (Art. 26(8)); the resilience tests every {{TEST_MONTHS}} months are the testing DORA asks for. A notice from {{AUTHORITY}} naming the entity supersedes this decision, and the first test is then planned with the authority.",
      "consequences": "• No red-team test on production and no attestation to keep.\n• A notice from the authority starts a project of months: the scope review should ask whether one is likely.",
      "alternatives": "1. A voluntary TIBER-style test: realistic assurance, at a cost DORA does not ask for.\n2. Waiting without a record: the notice then finds no owner.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full"
        ],
        "TLPT": [
          "not-named"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-report-runbook",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Major incidents filed by our own on-call",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 780,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "At least two people trained to file with {{AUTHORITY}} are on the on-call rota around the clock, paged as the Observability pack decides; the runbook docs/dora/incident-reporting.md names the authority, its reporting channel, the thresholds of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1772, the deadlines of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301 and the templates of Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302. The on-call engineer classifies within {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} hours of awareness and the filer submits. Once a year the filers fill the templates in a dry run from a past incident.",
      "consequences": "• The reports stay in the hands of the people who know the incident.\n• Two trained filers on every rota is a real cost for a small team.\n• The dry run is the only practice; a real major incident may be the first filing.",
      "alternatives": "1. A reporting service provider: filing at any hour bought in, with a hand-over to get right in the first hours.\n2. The compliance officer files alone: one person between the entity and a missed deadline.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REPORTING": [
          "runbook"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-report-outsourced",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Major incidents filed by a reporting provider",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 910,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "A reporting service provider files the reports with {{AUTHORITY}} under DORA Art. 19(5), under a contract that binds it to the time limits of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301 at any hour and names who at the provider receives the hand-over. The on-call engineer classifies within {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} hours of awareness and hands over through the channel in the runbook docs/dora/incident-reporting.md, which also names the authority, the thresholds and the deadlines. The entity stays fully responsible for the reports.",
      "consequences": "• Filing at any hour without training filers in-house.\n• The hand-over is the weak point: facts lost or late in it are the entity's fault, not the provider's.\n• The provider is itself an ICT third-party service and needs a register entry.",
      "alternatives": "1. Our own on-call filers: no hand-over, at the cost of training and rotas.\n2. A group company files for the whole group: one team, as long as it is reachable at any hour.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REPORTING": [
          "outsourced"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-register-model",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Register of information beside the model",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 1040,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The register entries for this system are kept in compliance/dora/register.yaml, one per ICT third-party service, named by the external system's label in the architecture model, with the fields the register templates of Implementing Regulation (EU) 2024/2956 key on (arrangement reference, provider LEI or EUID, function, type of ICT service S01 to S19) plus criticality, data locations and, for critical or important functions, the exit plan and the Art. 30(3) clauses. Every pull request runs a script that fails when the system calls an external system with no complete entry. The compliance officer builds the yearly xBRL-CSV report for {{AUTHORITY}} from this file.",
      "consequences": "• A new integration cannot merge without its register entry: engineers and procurement meet in the pull request.\n• The file holds contract data in the code repository; who may read it follows the repository's access.\n• The report for the authority is built from a file, so a conversion step to the ESAs' format is still needed.",
      "alternatives": "1. A GRC tool reconciled with the model: one register for the whole entity, with an export to keep in step.\n2. A spreadsheet kept by procurement: no link to what the code calls, found incomplete at the yearly report.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REGISTER": [
          "model"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-register-grc",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Register of information in a GRC tool",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 1170,
      "concern": "eu-dora",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The organisation that runs this system may be a financial entity, or may sell ICT services to financial entities, and so be bound by the EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation (EU) 2022/2554), which applies since 17 January 2025. The forces: whether and how DORA applies is a legal reading of the organisation's licences and clients, and a wrong reading is found by the supervisor, not by the team; a major ICT-related incident has to reach the competent authority within hours of being classified, at night and at weekends, and the clock starts when the entity becomes aware of the incident, not when someone finds the runbook; the register of information has to list every ICT service bought, and every new integration in the code is a new entry that procurement may never hear of; resilience tests and threat-led penetration tests run on a regulatory cycle, not when the team has time; and the contracts with ICT providers must say what DORA requires, which engineers rarely read. The DORA delivery metrics (deployment frequency and the others) share only the name; they are the Delivery metrics pack.",
      "decision": "The register of information lives in the organisation's GRC or third-party risk tool, which produces the yearly report for {{AUTHORITY}}. A nightly job exports the register to reports/register-export.csv (columns provider, contract_reference, provider_id, ict_service_type, function_id, critical, data_locations, exit_plan, clauses_art_30_3, with provider holding the external system's label in the architecture model) and a script fails when the system calls an external system the export does not cover completely.",
      "consequences": "• One register for the whole entity, kept by the people who own the contracts.\n• Engineers find out about a missing entry from a red nightly job, not before the merge.\n• The export's columns are a contract between the GRC tool and the check; a renamed column breaks it.",
      "alternatives": "1. A register file in the repository: checked before the merge, but only for the systems whose repositories hold one.\n2. No reconciliation with the model: the register lists what procurement bought, not what the code calls.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REGISTER": [
          "grc"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-scope",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "DORA scope record current",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "compliance/dora/scope.yaml has a scope, a basis, a reviewer and a review date at most {{SCOPE_REVIEW_MONTHS}} months old",
      "description": "A script run in CI on every pull request and monthly on a schedule reads the scope record and fails when a field is missing or the last review is older than {{SCOPE_REVIEW_MONTHS}} months, so a stale scope shows up before the supervisor asks.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml, in a CI step\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/scope-check.mjs — node tools/dora/scope-check.mjs compliance/dora/scope.yaml\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MONTH_MS = 30.44 * 86_400_000\nconst scope = yaml.load(readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA }) ?? {}\nconst problems = ['scope', 'basis', 'reviewed', 'reviewed_by'].filter((k) => !scope[k]).map((k) => `no ${k}`)\nconst age = (Date.now() - new Date(scope.reviewed).getTime()) / MONTH_MS\nif (!(age <= {{SCOPE_REVIEW_MONTHS}})) problems.push(`last reviewed ${scope.reviewed}: more than {{SCOPE_REVIEW_MONTHS}} months ago`)\nif (problems.length) { console.error(`compliance/dora/scope.yaml: ${problems.join('; ')}`); process.exit(1) }\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-runbook",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Reporting runbook names authority and deadlines",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "docs/dora/incident-reporting.md names {{AUTHORITY}}, the three regulations, the 4-hour, 24-hour, 72-hour and one-month deadlines and clients, and has sections Classification, Reporting channel and Who files",
      "description": "A CI step searches the runbook for the phrases a filer needs and for its three sections; a runbook that loses a deadline or the name of the authority in an edit fails the pull request.\n\nTool: bash with grep, in a CI step\n\n```bash\n# CI: the incident reporting runbook names the authority, the classification rules, the deadlines and who files\nset -euo pipefail\nrunbook=docs/dora/incident-reporting.md\nmissing=0\nfor phrase in '{{AUTHORITY}}' 'Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1772' 'Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301' 'Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302' \\\n  'initial notification' '4 hours' '24 hours' 'intermediate report' '72 hours' 'final report' 'one month' 'clients'; do\n  grep -qiF -- \"$phrase\" \"$runbook\" || { echo \"$runbook does not name: $phrase\"; missing=1; }\ndone\nfor section in 'Classification' 'Reporting channel' 'Who files'; do\n  grep -qiE \"^#+ +$section\" \"$runbook\" || { echo \"$runbook has no section: $section\"; missing=1; }\ndone\nexit \"$missing\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-deadlines",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Incidents classified and reported on time",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "operational",
      "threshold": "0 incidents classified later than {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} hours after awareness; 0 major incidents with an initial notification, first intermediate report or final report past its time limit, or with affected clients and no time they were informed",
      "description": "A daily CI job reads the incident tool's export (reports/incidents.json: id, major, aware_at, classified_at, initial_at, intermediate_at as a list, final_at, clients_affected, clients_informed_at; ISO 8601 UTC, null when not yet done) and computes each time limit of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/301 Art. 5(1) and (2); a report not yet filed counts as late once its time limit has passed. The weekend and bank-holiday extension of Art. 5(4) is not applied, so the check is stricter than the rule; a month is a calendar month as jq's mktime normalises it (31 January plus one month is 3 March).\n\nTool: jq 1.7 in a bash CI step, over the export of incident.io, PagerDuty or Jira Service Management\n\n```bash\n# CI, daily: reports/incidents.json is the incident tool's export (ISO 8601 UTC times, null when not yet done)\nset -euo pipefail\nlate=$(jq -r --argjson classify_h {{CLASSIFY_HOURS}} '\n  def t: if . == null then null else sub(\"\\\\.[0-9]+\"; \"\") | fromdateiso8601 end;\n  def late($due; $at): if $at == null then now > $due else $at > $due end;\n  .[] | . as $i | (.aware_at | t) as $aware | (.classified_at | t) as $cls | (.initial_at | t) as $init\n  | ((.intermediate_at // []) | map(t)) as $mids\n  | [ (select(late($aware + $classify_h * 3600; $cls)) | \"classified late\"),\n      (select(.major) | (if $cls - $aware > 86400 then $cls + 14400 else ([$cls + 14400, $aware + 86400] | min) end) as $due\n        | select(late($due; $init)) | \"initial notification late\"),\n      (select(.major and $init != null) | select(late($init + 259200; $mids[0])) | \"intermediate report late\"),\n      (select(.major and ($mids | length) > 0) | select(late($mids | max | gmtime | .[1] += 1 | mktime; .final_at | t)) | \"final report late\"),\n      (select(.major and .clients_affected and .clients_informed_at == null) | \"clients not informed\") ]\n  | select(length > 0) | \"\\($i.id): \\(join(\", \"))\"' reports/incidents.json)\n[ -z \"$late\" ] || { echo \"$late\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-register-model",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every called external system in the register file",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 external systems called by an internal system in the architecture model without a register entry; 0 entries missing contract_reference, provider_id, function_id, data_locations, a true or false critical, or an ict_service_type of S01 to S19; 0 critical entries without exit_plan and clauses_art_30_3",
      "description": "A script on every pull request reads the architecture model folder (nodes/**/*.md with YAML front matter, a folder's _index.md being the parent of the files beside it, and relations.json), finds each system an internal system or one of its parts calls with an interacts relation and that is marked external, and looks up its entry by label in compliance/dora/register.yaml. An entry looks like: { provider: Partner, contract_reference: CTR-2025-014, provider_id: <LEI or EUID>, ict_service_type: S19, function_id: F-PAYMENTS, critical: true, data_locations: [DE, IE], exit_plan: docs/dora/exit/partner.md, clauses_art_30_3: [service levels, notice periods, business continuity testing, TLPT participation, audit rights, exit transition period] }.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml, in a CI step\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/register-check.mjs — node tools/dora/register-check.mjs architecture compliance/dora/register.yaml\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst [model, registerFile] = process.argv.slice(2)\nconst nodes = new Map(), folders = new Map()\nfor (const file of readdirSync(join(model, 'nodes'), { recursive: true }).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))) {\n  const front = yaml.load(readFileSync(join(model, 'nodes', file), 'utf8').split(/^---$/m)[1])\n  const index = file.endsWith('_index.md')\n  nodes.set(front.id, { ...front, folder: index ? dirname(dirname(file)) : dirname(file) })\n  if (index) folders.set(dirname(file), front.id)\n}\nconst systemOf = (id) => { for (let n = nodes.get(id); n; n = nodes.get(folders.get(n.folder))) if (n.type === 'system') return n }\nconst called = new Set(JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(model, 'relations.json'), 'utf8')).filter((r) => r.relationType === 'interacts')\n  .map((r) => [systemOf(r.sourceId), systemOf(r.targetId)]).filter(([s, t]) => s && !s.external && t?.external).map(([, t]) => t.label))\nconst register = new Map((yaml.load(readFileSync(registerFile, 'utf8')) ?? []).map((e) => [e.provider, e]))\nconst problems = [...called].flatMap((label) => {\n  const e = register.get(label)\n  if (!e) return [`${label}: called in the model, no register entry`]\n  const keys = ['contract_reference', 'provider_id', 'function_id', 'data_locations', ...(e.critical ? ['exit_plan', 'clauses_art_30_3'] : [])]\n  return [...keys.filter((k) => !e[k]?.length), ...(typeof e.critical === 'boolean' ? [] : ['critical: true or false']),\n    ...(/^S(0[1-9]|1\\d)$/.test(e.ict_service_type) ? [] : ['ict_service_type: S01 to S19'])].map((k) => `${label}: no ${k}`)\n})\nif (problems.length) { console.error(problems.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REGISTER": [
          "model"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-register-grc",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every called external system in the GRC register",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 external systems called by an internal system in the architecture model missing from the register export; 0 rows with an empty contract_reference, provider_id, ict_service_type, function_id or data_locations; 0 critical rows with an empty exit_plan or clauses_art_30_3",
      "description": "A nightly job exports the register from the GRC tool to reports/register-export.csv with the columns the decision names, then a script reads the architecture model folder (nodes/**/*.md with YAML front matter, _index.md for a folder's own node, relations.json), finds each external system an internal system calls, and fails when the export has no complete row for it.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml and csv-parse 5, in a scheduled CI job after the GRC tool's CSV export\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/register-grc-check.mjs — node tools/dora/register-grc-check.mjs architecture reports/register-export.csv\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\nimport { parse } from 'csv-parse/sync'\nconst [model, exportFile] = process.argv.slice(2)\nconst nodes = new Map(), folders = new Map()\nfor (const file of readdirSync(join(model, 'nodes'), { recursive: true }).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))) {\n  const front = yaml.load(readFileSync(join(model, 'nodes', file), 'utf8').split(/^---$/m)[1])\n  const index = file.endsWith('_index.md')\n  nodes.set(front.id, { ...front, folder: index ? dirname(dirname(file)) : dirname(file) })\n  if (index) folders.set(dirname(file), front.id)\n}\nconst systemOf = (id) => { for (let n = nodes.get(id); n; n = nodes.get(folders.get(n.folder))) if (n.type === 'system') return n }\nconst called = new Set(JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(model, 'relations.json'), 'utf8')).filter((r) => r.relationType === 'interacts')\n  .map((r) => [systemOf(r.sourceId), systemOf(r.targetId)]).filter(([s, t]) => s && !s.external && t?.external).map(([, t]) => t.label))\nconst rows = new Map(parse(readFileSync(exportFile, 'utf8'), { columns: true, skip_empty_lines: true }).map((r) => [r.provider, r]))\nconst problems = [...called].flatMap((label) => {\n  const r = rows.get(label)\n  if (!r) return [`${label}: called in the model, not in the GRC tool's register`]\n  const critical = r.critical === 'true'\n  return ['contract_reference', 'provider_id', 'ict_service_type', 'function_id', 'data_locations', ...(critical ? ['exit_plan', 'clauses_art_30_3'] : [])]\n    .filter((k) => !r[k]?.trim()).map((k) => `${label}: ${k} empty in the register`)\n})\nif (problems.length) { console.error(problems.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ],
        "REGISTER": [
          "grc"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-tests",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Resilience tests on the cycle, findings fixed",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 systems marked critical without a test in the last {{TEST_MONTHS}} months; 0 tests without types and tester; 0 findings open past their due date",
      "description": "A monthly CI job reads compliance/dora/resilience-tests.yaml, one item per system named by its label in the architecture model: { system: Shop, critical: true, tests: [{ date: 2026-05-12, types: [vulnerability scans, scenario-based tests, penetration testing], tester: external, findings: [{ id: RT-12, priority: high, due: 2026-07-12, closed: 2026-07-01 }] }] }. It fails on an overdue test, a test that names no types or tester, and a finding open past its due date.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml, in a scheduled CI job\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/tests-check.mjs — node tools/dora/tests-check.mjs compliance/dora/resilience-tests.yaml\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MONTH_MS = 30.44 * 86_400_000\nconst systems = yaml.load(readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA }) ?? []\nconst problems = systems.flatMap((s) => {\n  const tests = s.tests ?? []\n  const last = Math.max(0, ...tests.map((t) => new Date(t.date).getTime()))\n  return [\n    ...(s.critical && (Date.now() - last) / MONTH_MS > {{TEST_MONTHS}} ? [`${s.system}: no resilience test in the last {{TEST_MONTHS}} months`] : []),\n    ...tests.filter((t) => !t.types?.length || !t.tester).map((t) => `${s.system}: test of ${t.date} names no types or tester`),\n    ...tests.flatMap((t) => t.findings ?? []).filter((f) => !f.closed && new Date(f.due) < new Date())\n      .map((f) => `${s.system}: finding ${f.id} (${f.priority}) open past ${f.due}`),\n  ]\n})\nif (problems.length) { console.error(problems.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full",
          "simplified"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-tlpt",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Threat-led penetration test not overdue",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The last threat-led penetration test ended at most {{TLPT_MONTHS}} months ago, has an attestation date, and names the functions it covered",
      "description": "A monthly CI job reads the tlpt block of compliance/dora/scope.yaml ({ last_test_end: 2025-11-20, attestation: 2026-02-03, functions: [payments, card issuing] }) and fails when the test is overdue, has no attestation, or names no functions.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml, in a scheduled CI job\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/tlpt-check.mjs — node tools/dora/tlpt-check.mjs compliance/dora/scope.yaml\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst MONTH_MS = 30.44 * 86_400_000\nconst { tlpt } = yaml.load(readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf8'), { schema: yaml.JSON_SCHEMA }) ?? {}\nconst problems = []\nif (!tlpt?.functions?.length) problems.push('no critical or important functions named for the test')\nconst age = (Date.now() - new Date(tlpt?.last_test_end).getTime()) / MONTH_MS\nif (!(age <= {{TLPT_MONTHS}})) problems.push(`last TLPT ended ${tlpt?.last_test_end ?? 'never'}: more than {{TLPT_MONTHS}} months ago`)\nif (tlpt?.last_test_end && !tlpt.attestation) problems.push('no attestation from the authority for the last TLPT')\nif (problems.length) { console.error(`TLPT: ${problems.join('; ')}`); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "full"
        ],
        "TLPT": [
          "named"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-client-notice",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Clients told in time with the facts",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "operational",
      "threshold": "0 incidents on a client-facing service where clients were told later than {{CLIENT_NOTICE_HOURS}} hours after detection, or not yet told past that; 0 notices missing clients_affected, duration_minutes, downtime_minutes, data_losses, member_states or economic_impact_eur",
      "description": "A daily CI job reads the incident tool's export (reports/incidents.json: id, client_impact, detected_at, clients_notified_at, and the notice sent as an object) and fails on a late notice or one that lacks a fact the clients need for their own classification.\n\nTool: jq 1.7 in a bash CI step, over the export of incident.io, PagerDuty or Jira Service Management\n\n```bash\n# CI, daily: reports/incidents.json is the incident tool's export; client_impact marks an incident on a service financial-entity clients use\nset -euo pipefail\nlate=$(jq -r --argjson hours {{CLIENT_NOTICE_HOURS}} '\n  def t: sub(\"\\\\.[0-9]+\"; \"\") | fromdateiso8601;\n  .[] | select(.client_impact) | . as $i | ($i.detected_at | t + $hours * 3600) as $due\n  | [ (select(if $i.clients_notified_at == null then now > $due else ($i.clients_notified_at | t) > $due end) | \"clients told late\"),\n      (([\"clients_affected\", \"duration_minutes\", \"downtime_minutes\", \"data_losses\", \"member_states\", \"economic_impact_eur\"]\n        - ($i.notice // {} | keys)) | select(length > 0) | \"notice lacks \\(join(\", \"))\") ]\n  | select(length > 0) | \"\\($i.id): \\(join(\"; \"))\"' reports/incidents.json)\n[ -z \"$late\" ] || { echo \"$late\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "provider"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-subcontractors",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every called external system on the subcontractor list",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 external systems called by an internal system in the architecture model without an entry in compliance/dora/subcontractors.yaml; 0 entries missing legal_entity, services, data_locations or supports",
      "description": "A script on every pull request reads the architecture model folder (nodes/**/*.md with YAML front matter, _index.md for a folder's own node, relations.json), finds each external system an internal system calls, and looks it up by label in the subcontractor list: { provider: Partner, legal_entity: Partner Payments GmbH, services: [card payment authorisation], data_locations: [DE], supports: [order checkout] }.\n\nTool: Node 20 with js-yaml, in a CI step\n\n```js\n// tools/dora/subcontractors-check.mjs — node tools/dora/subcontractors-check.mjs architecture compliance/dora/subcontractors.yaml\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\nimport yaml from 'js-yaml'\n\nconst [model, listFile] = process.argv.slice(2)\nconst nodes = new Map(), folders = new Map()\nfor (const file of readdirSync(join(model, 'nodes'), { recursive: true }).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))) {\n  const front = yaml.load(readFileSync(join(model, 'nodes', file), 'utf8').split(/^---$/m)[1])\n  const index = file.endsWith('_index.md')\n  nodes.set(front.id, { ...front, folder: index ? dirname(dirname(file)) : dirname(file) })\n  if (index) folders.set(dirname(file), front.id)\n}\nconst systemOf = (id) => { for (let n = nodes.get(id); n; n = nodes.get(folders.get(n.folder))) if (n.type === 'system') return n }\nconst called = new Set(JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(model, 'relations.json'), 'utf8')).filter((r) => r.relationType === 'interacts')\n  .map((r) => [systemOf(r.sourceId), systemOf(r.targetId)]).filter(([s, t]) => s && !s.external && t?.external).map(([, t]) => t.label))\nconst listed = new Map((yaml.load(readFileSync(listFile, 'utf8')) ?? []).map((s) => [s.provider, s]))\nconst problems = [...called].flatMap((label) => {\n  const s = listed.get(label)\n  if (!s) return [`${label}: called in the model, not on the subcontractor list given to clients`]\n  return ['legal_entity', 'services', 'data_locations', 'supports'].filter((k) => !s[k]?.length).map((k) => `${label}: no ${k}`)\n})\nif (problems.length) { console.error(problems.join('\\n')); process.exit(1) }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "provider"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-addendum",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Contract addendum has every DORA clause",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "contracts/dora-addendum.md has a heading for each of the 14 clauses of DORA Art. 30(2) and 30(3) it checks",
      "description": "A CI step looks for a heading per clause in the addendum offered to clients: the nine of Art. 30(2) and, for critical or important functions, notice and reporting, business continuity, TLPT, audit and exit of Art. 30(3) (its service levels share the Service levels section). A clause dropped in an edit fails the pull request.\n\nTool: bash with grep, in a CI step\n\n```bash\n# CI: the DORA addendum offered to financial-entity clients has a section for each clause of Art. 30(2) and 30(3)\nset -euo pipefail\naddendum=contracts/dora-addendum.md\nmissing=0\nwhile IFS= read -r clause; do\n  grep -qiE \"^#+ +.*$clause\" \"$addendum\" || { echo \"$addendum has no section on: $clause\"; missing=1; }\ndone <<'CLAUSES'\nDescription of services and subcontracting\nLocations of services and data\nData availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality\nAccess to, recovery and return of data\nService levels\nAssistance with ICT incidents\nCooperation with authorities\nTermination rights\nSecurity awareness and training\nNotice and reporting obligations\nBusiness continuity\nThreat-led penetration testing\nAudit and inspection rights\nExit strategy and transition period\nCLAUSES\nexit \"$missing\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "SCOPE": [
          "provider"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
